course: english
lesson: 56
title: Olympiad: word puzzles — anagrams, palindromes, word ladders
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Word puzzles treat letters like pieces in a game: you rearrange them, reverse them or swap them, and every answer must be a real word.
explain: An anagram uses exactly the same letters in a new order: LISTEN becomes SILENT, and DORMITORY becomes DIRTY ROOM.
explain: A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards (level, noon, Nan), and so can a whole sentence if you ignore spaces, capitals and punctuation: "Was it a car or a cat I saw?"
explain: In a word ladder you change one letter at a time and every step must be a real word: COLD, CORD, CARD, WARD, WARM.
explain: Picture Kai stuck on HEAD to TAIL at HEAL: the next step is TEAL (a blue-green colour, and also a duck), then TELL, TALL, TAIL; good puzzlers try changing each letter position in turn until a real word appears.
fact: An anagram uses all the letters of a word or phrase, each exactly once, in a new order.
fact: LISTEN is an anagram of SILENT, and the same letters also make ENLIST and TINSEL.
fact: DORMITORY is an anagram of DIRTY ROOM.
fact: A palindrome reads the same forwards and backwards, such as mum, dad, Nan, level and noon.
fact: "Was it a car or a cat I saw?" is a palindrome sentence once spaces, capitals and punctuation are ignored.
fact: A word ladder changes exactly one letter per step, and every step must be a real word: HEAD, HEAL, TEAL, TELL, TALL, TAIL.
fact: "Facetious" (joking at the wrong moment) contains the five vowels a, e, i, o, u in that order, and so does "abstemious".
fact: A pangram uses every letter of the alphabet; "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog" is the famous one, used to test keyboards and to show what a typeface looks like.
fact: A compound word joins two words, like butter + fly = butterfly; a chain of them can go flyover, overtime, timetable.
fact: The seven letters of TEACHER also make CHEATER and HECTARE, and shorter words such as cheat, reach, each and heart.
fact: A spoonerism swaps the first sounds of two words, as in "a well-boiled icicle" for "a well-oiled bicycle"; it is named after William Spooner, an Oxford teacher who was said to make such slips.
fact: "Bruv" is London slang for brother or mate.
exercise: Rearrange LISTEN to make a word meaning quiet. | SILENT | TINSEL | ENLIST | INLETS
exercise: Which is an anagram of DORMITORY? | DIRTY ROOM | DORM STORY | MIRROR TOY | ROOM TIDY
exercise: Is "Was it a car or a cat I saw?" a palindrome? | Yes, ignoring spaces and punctuation | No, because it ends with a question mark | No, because "car" and "cat" are different | Yes, but only when it is read out aloud
exercise: Grace finds Mum, Dad, Nan, level and noon. How many of them are palindromes? | All five | Four | Three | Two
exercise: Which word is NOT a palindrome? | tent | kayak | radar | refer
exercise: Isla's ladder goes COLD, CORD, CARD, WARD, WARM. Is it legal? | Yes, one letter changes each time | No, she changes two letters once | No, CORD is not a real word | No, a ladder needs ten steps
exercise: Which ladder from HEAD to TAIL is legal? | HEAD, HEAL, TEAL, TELL, TALL, TAIL | HEAD, HEAL, TALL, TAIL | HEAD, HEAT, TEAT, TAIL | HEAD, HEAL, HAIL, TAIL
exercise: Kai is stuck at HEAL. What is TEAL, the next step? | A blue-green colour, and a duck | A kind of tea that is drunk cold | A spelling mistake for "tell" | A made-up word that is not allowed
exercise: Which word has all five vowels, a, e, i, o, u, in that order? | facetious | education | sequoia | favourite
exercise: Why do typists use "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"? | It uses every letter, so every key is tested | It is the shortest sentence in all of English | The fox is the lucky animal of all typists | It has no vowels, so it is tricky to type
exercise: What is a sentence that uses every letter called? | A pangram | A palindrome | An anagram | A spoonerism
exercise: BUTTER + FLY = BUTTERFLY. Which chain of compound words carries on correctly from FLY? | flyover, overtime, timetable | flytable, tablefly, flyfly | flyer, butter, bread | fly, flies, flew
exercise: Which word uses all seven letters of TEACHER? | CHEATER | CHEAT | TEACH | REACH
exercise: Which of these can you NOT make from the letters of TEACHER? | THREAT | HEART | REACH | EACH
exercise: Swap the first sounds in "a well-boiled icicle". What do you get? | a well-oiled bicycle | a boiled well icicle | a well-boiled bicycle | an icy boiled well
exercise: Who are spoonerisms named after? | William Spooner, an Oxford teacher | A cook who kept dropping spoons | A spoon factory in Sheffield | A comic about a talking spoon
test: Kai says FLYTABLE is a word; his sister says, "Nah, that's not a word, bruv." Who is right? | His sister: it is not a real word | Kai: any two words joined make a word | Both: it counts as half a word | Neither: chains don't need real words
test: Your mate says, "An anagram can drop a letter if it's hard, innit." Put him right. | Every letter must be used exactly once | One letter may be dropped if it is hard | You may drop up to two letters | You may add in any letter you like
test: Change CAT into DOG, one letter at a time. Which ladder is legal? | CAT, COT, COG, DOG | CAT, CAG, DOG | CAT, DOT, DOG | CAT, BAT, BOG, DOG
test: What would happen to the palindrome "level" if its last letter became "s"? | It would stop being a palindrome | It would still be a palindrome | It would be an anagram of "level" | It would turn into a pangram
test: Nan from Yorkshire: "Ey up, love, 'noon' reads t'same both ways, dun't it?" ("Ey up" means hello; "dun't it" means doesn't it.) Is she right? | Yes, noon is a palindrome | No, noon has three letters | No, only names can be palindromes | Yes, but only in capital letters
test: A cartoon says "dad" and "add" are both palindromes. Fix it. | "dad" is, but "add" backwards is "dda" | They are both palindromes, so it's right | Neither of them is a palindrome at all | "add" is a palindrome, but "dad" is not
test: Which pair are anagrams of each other? | heart and earth | heart and hearth | heat and earth | hear and heart
test: A pal says, "Any long sentence is a pangram, no cap." ("No cap" means no lie.) Which one is a pangram? | The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. | The big brown bear sat on a very lazy log. | Twelve happy children played in the snowy park. | A cat and a dog ran over the green hill today.
test: Why can't a word ladder go from CAT to HORSE? | The words have different numbers of letters | Horses and cats are not related animals | HORSE has an S in it, and CAT has not | It can, in exactly six careful steps
test: Which word uses exactly the letters of TEACHER? | HECTARE | CHARTER | THEATRE | HEATER
test: An old teacher is said to have told a student, "You have hissed my mystery lectures." What did he mean? | You have missed my history lectures | You have kissed my mister lectures | You have hissed at all my lectures | You have mist my hysterical lectures
exam: Identify the term for a word or sentence that reads identically forwards and backwards. | A palindrome | An anagram | A pangram | A spoonerism
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes an anagram of "listen" bearing the meaning "quiet". | Silent | Tinsel | Enlist | Inlets
exam: It is given that in a word ladder exactly one letter may be altered at each step. Select the sequence that complies. | cold, cord, card, ward, warm | cold, card, ward, warm | cold, word, warm | cold, cord, ward, warm
exam: Determine the term denoting a sentence comprising every letter of the alphabet. | A pangram | A palindrome | A lipogram | A kenning
